function faqs() {
  const entries = [
    ['What is the 12 axes political test?', 'It’s a free questionnaire that organizes your political preferences into twelve separate dimensions, including the economy, personal freedom, culture, and the environment. It is designed for reflection, not for assigning a definitive political identity.'],
    ['Is the test really free?', 'Yes. Both test lengths, all twelve scores, explanations, and image downloads are free. There are no accounts, premium reports, or payment steps.'],
    ['How long does the political test take?', 'The quick version has 48 questions and takes about 6–9 minutes. The full version has 72 questions and takes about 10–15 minutes. Both cover all twelve axes and include your complete results for free. You can return to earlier questions or resume saved progress in the same browser.'],
    ['How is a 12 axes test different from a political compass?', 'This test reports twelve separate dimensions rather than a single position on a two-axis chart. You can lean toward different poles on different subjects. Other political quizzes use their own questions and scales, so scores are not interchangeable.'],
    ['Is this a scientific assessment?', 'No. This original questionnaire has not been independently validated. Scores reflect your answers to a limited set of statements, not an objective measurement, diagnosis, or prediction of your behavior.'],
    ['What happens to my answers?', 'While a test is in progress, its answers are saved only in this browser. After completion, we keep the scores and a small explanation summary, including at most two answer examples per axis; the complete answer list is no longer saved. Optional Google Analytics and Microsoft Clarity run only after you allow analytics. We measure page visits and basic test actions without sending your answers or scores as analytics data; session recordings exclude questionnaire and result pages. You can clear local test data or change analytics preferences in the footer. Cloudflare still receives normal website requests.'],
    ['Can I save or share my result?', 'Yes. Download an image, print your result, or copy an optional share link. Share links contain only the twelve scores, questionnaire version, and quick/full mode, not answer examples or contribution counts. Anyone with the link can view those scores.'],
    ['Why does an older result have 36 questions?', 'New v2 tests have 48 or 72 statements with balanced scoring directions. Saved v1 sessions can finish with their original 36 or 72 statements. Old results keep their original scores and version; they are not recalculated or directly comparable with v2.'],
    ['Why might my result change?', 'Different statements, your interpretation, and your views at the time can all change a score. A short test is especially sensitive to individual answers. Read each dimension as a conversation starter rather than a fixed label.']
  ];
  return entries.map(([question, answer]) => `<details><summary>${question}<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>${answer}</p></details>`).join('');
}

export function setupPage() {
  return `<section class="container setup-section"><span class="eyebrow">Your political perspective</span><h1>Take the<br><span>12 Axes Political Test</span></h1><p class="setup-description">Every version covers all twelve dimensions. Pick a pace that works for you, give your honest answers, and discover your complete profile.</p><div id="resume-slot"></div>${versionCards()}<div class="before-start">${interfaceIcon('shield')}<p><strong>No right answers. No account needed.</strong> Neutral is a valid response. Progress is saved in this browser, and you can return to earlier questions at any time. This is an educational tool, not a scientifically validated assessment.</p></div><p class="setup-legal">By starting, you agree to our <a href="/terms">Terms of use</a>. Read our <a href="/privacy">Privacy policy</a> to learn how your local data is stored.</p></section><article class="container prose"><h2>Which version should you choose?</h2><p>The quick test has 48 statements, four for each axis, two in each scoring direction, and takes about 6–9 minutes. Choose it for a first look at your political priorities. The full test has 72 statements, six per axis, and takes about 10–15 minutes. It gives you more statements to consider within each subject. Both versions are free and include all twelve scores, explanations, image downloads, and optional sharing.</p><p>More questions do not turn this into a scientifically validated assessment. The quick result is especially sensitive to a single answer, and both versions reflect the wording of this original questionnaire. Read the <a href="/methodology">scoring method and limitations</a> for context.</p><h2>How to answer</h2><p>Read each statement and choose from strongly agree, agree, neutral / unsure, disagree, or strongly disagree. Answer for your own views, rather than what you think a political group expects. Neutral is a valid response. You can go back to revise answers and turn off automatic advance if you want more time.</p><h2>What your result shows</h2><p>You receive a radar chart, several highlighted tendencies rather than an identity label, and twelve paired scores covering economics, civil liberties, decision-making, global relations, security, migration, social change, religion, the environment, criminal justice, distribution of power, and technology. <a href="/axes">Explore the twelve axes and their two poles</a> before or after the test.</p><p>Each pair adds up to 100%. These percentages summarize your answers; they are not population rankings, probabilities, or a match to a political party. A larger radar shape is not a better result.</p><h2>Save your progress and your result</h2><p>Progress is saved in this browser when local storage is available. Return to this page to resume on the same browser and device; starting another test replaces saved progress. If storage is unavailable, keep the current tab open.</p><p>After finishing, you can download a result image, print or save a PDF, or copy a share link. Sharing is optional: anyone with the complete link can read your twelve scores, and the link cannot be revoked. The link contains only scores, questionnaire version, and test mode. Local contribution counts and the maximum of two answer examples per axis are excluded. The full answer list is no longer saved after completion. Use the footer to clear saved test data whenever you choose.</p></article>`;
}

